==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}}

Gustav Flaubert, the French writer, advanced the idea of writer's finding 'le mot juste' i.e. the right word, which he considered as the key mean to achieve quality in literary art.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/məʊˈʒuːst/}} {{qualifier|or as French, below}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|sg=[[mot]] [[juste]]|pl2=mots justes}}

# The perfectly appropriate word or phrase for the situation.
#* {{quote-book
|year=1960
|author={{w|P. G. Wodehouse}}
|title={{w|Jeeves in the Offing}}
|section=chapter V
|passage=She legged it, and for a moment silence reigned. Then Bobbie said, “Phew!” and I agreed that “Phew!” was the '''''mot juste'''''.}}

